\begin{abstract}
Execution-free verification for LLM-based code repair can be formulated as per-test binary classification (\cls{}), execution output generation (\cwm{}), or trajectory-level patch scoring, yet no controlled comparison exists across formulations. We evaluate all three on the same source data with formulation-appropriate label granularity at controlled model sizes (4--8B LoRA fine-tuning).

At function level, formulations achieve near-equivalent accuracy (\cls{} 88.4\% vs.\ \cwm{} 87.2\% at 4B, 5 seeds; Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) \cls{}--\cwm{} equivalence at $p{=}0.007$). At repository level, divergence amplifies to 19--26\pp{}, with \cwm{} falling below majority baseline due to format non-compliance and output space complexity. Cross-model validation (Qwen3, DeepSeek-Coder) confirms this pattern is consistent across both families but model-contingent in magnitude.

Two findings generalize. First, per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity (which tests fail) at the best or comparable accuracy across formulations, with the advantage concentrating in multi-failure instances. Second, formulations exhibit complementary errors (oracle ensemble: 90.63\%). \cls{} achieves the highest or equivalent accuracy across conditions, but aggregation penalty and error complementarity motivate complexity-aware routing.
\end{abstract}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented the first controlled comparison of three execution-free verification formulations (\cls{}, \cwm{}, \traj{}). \cls{} achieves the highest per-test accuracy across all tested conditions; at variant level, aggregation penalty makes the advantage configuration-dependent, motivating complexity-aware routing.

At function level, \cls{} and \cwm{} achieve equivalent accuracy (TOST $p{=}0.007$); \cls{} shows a small advantage over \traj{} ($+$1.89\pp{}). At repository level under our training protocol, divergence amplifies to 19--26\pp{}; cross-model validation confirms this is consistent across both families but model-contingent in magnitude (Qwen3: ${\sim}$5/22\pp{}; DeepSeek: 4--12/25--30\pp{} for \cls{}/\cwm{}). Seed variance and evaluation protocol add further sensitivity (${\sim}$9\pp{} for the same \cwm{} checkpoint).

Two properties generalize across models. First, per-test classification provides diagnostic granularity at comparable or better accuracy at function level; at repo level, aggregation penalty is configuration-dependent (1.36--19\pp{}). Second, classification yields 217$\times$ throughput over generation. Error complementarity (oracle ensemble: 90.63\%) further motivates instance-level routing.

\section*{Limitations}

Most repo-level comparisons use a single seed; training data comprises synthetic mutations~\cite{andrews2005mutation,just2014mutants}. Our \cwm{} baseline uses 4--8B LoRA fine-tuning; the original 32B Code World Model with execution trace mid-training~\cite{meta2025cwm} may exhibit different scaling behavior at repository level. \traj{} training uses variant-level labels (386 instances at repo level vs.\ ${\sim}$70K for \cls{}/\cwm{}); this asymmetry is inherent to the formulation design but limits direct training-data-controlled comparison. Multi-seed replication and complexity-aware routing are natural extensions; a re-repair pilot ($n{=}44$, $p{=}0.42$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_analysis}(j)) found no benefit from per-test diagnostics. Extended discussion appears in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.
